# Findings `task audit:container` has looked at and accepted, with the reason and
# the condition that would take each one back out.
#
# This file exists so the scan can run at MEDIUM rather than HIGH. A gate set above
# a finding cannot tell "we understand this" from "nobody has looked" — and the two
# entries below are exactly the kind that get lost under a threshold: one is a
# genuine upstream constraint, the other is a test fixture. Written down, they cost
# a reader thirty seconds; unwritten, they cost a threshold that hides the next one.
# (`charts/weebo-notify/.trivyignore` does the same job for the Helm scan.)

# GHSA-wrw7-89jp-8q8g / RUSTSEC-2024-0429 — unsoundness in `glib`'s
# `VariantStrIter` iterator impls, fixed in glib 0.20.
#
# NOT FIXABLE FROM HERE, and not a vulnerability: RustSec classes it `unsound`, and
# `cargo audit` reports zero vulnerabilities for this lockfile. It reaches us only
# through `web/src-tauri`, where `gtk 0.18.2` is pinned by Tauri 2.11.5 itself —
# via `tao`, `wry`, `webkit2gtk`, `muda` and `libappindicator` — because Tauri v2 on
# Linux is built on the GTK3 bindings. That whole binding set is unmaintained
# upstream (the gtk-rs project moved to GTK4), so "upgrade glib" is not a move that
# exists until Tauri's Linux stack does.
#
# Scope, stated plainly: that crate is the desktop shell (M5). No gate compiles it,
# no release ships it, and nothing in the deployed server or web image links it.
#
# REVISIT IF: Tauri moves off GTK3, or this stops being `unsound` and becomes a
# vulnerability with a reachable path.
GHSA-wrw7-89jp-8q8g

# jwt-token in `crates/notify-adapters/oidc/src/federation.rs` — a JWT literal
# inside a `#[cfg(test)]` assertion, signed with a key generated in the same test.
# The same finding is allowlisted in `.gitleaks.toml`, with the fuller reasoning
# about why a scan that always reports something is a scan nobody reads.
#
# REVISIT IF: that file ever carries a token minted against a real issuer.
jwt-token
